4. Compute the mean-squared sum of squares (MSS) for the group means and the mean-squared square of standard (SDSS) for the sample means.
5. Compute the F-statistic, which is the ratio of the mean-squared sum of squares (MSS) to the mean-squared square of standard (SDSS).
6. Determine the critical F-statistic for the given significance level (e.g., 0.05) and degrees of freedom (1 for the group means and 19 for the sample means).
7. Compare the calculated F-statistic to the critical F-statistic. If the calculated F-statistic is greater than the critical F-statistic, the student can reject the null hypothesis and conclude that there are significant differences in the mean height of wheat plants between the four groups.

