Chronic stress can lead to a decrease in NK cell activity, which can increase the risk of infections, cancer, and other diseases. NK cell activity is influenced by a complex interplay of factors, including the presence of pro-inflammatory cytokines, the balance between pro- and anti-inflammatory molecules, and the presence of specific receptors on the surface of NK cells. NK cell activity is also influenced by the local environment of the cells, such as the presence of other immune cells and the presence of specific molecules on the surface of the cells.

