</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we need to use the standard molar entropy values (S°) of the reactants and products. The balanced chemical equation for the reaction is:

CO(g) + 1/2 O2(g) → CO2(g)

Since we are burning 3 moles of CO, we can multiply the equation by 3 to get the balanced equation for the reaction in question:

3 CO(g) + 3/2 O2(g) → 3 CO2(g)

Now, we can use the standard molar entropy values (S°) at 298 K to calculate the change in entropy (ΔS):

ΔS = ΣnS°(products) - ΣnS°(reactants)

From standard thermodynamic tables, we can find the standard molar entropies (J/mol·K) at 298 K for the given compounds:

S°(CO) = 135.6 J/mol·K
S°(O2) = 205.0 J/mol·K
S°(CO2) = 213.7 J/mol·K

Now, we can plug these values into the equation:

ΔS = [3 * S°(CO2)] - [3 * S°(CO) + (3/2) * S°(O2)]

ΔS = [3 * 213.7 J/mol·K] - [3 * 135.6 J/mol·K + (3/2) * 205.0 J/mol·K]

ΔS = 641.1 J/mol·K - [406.8 J/mol·K + 307.5 J/mol·K]

ΔS = 641.1 J/mol·K - 714.3 J/mol·K

ΔS = -73.2 J/mol·K

The change in entropy for the reaction is -73.2 J/mol·K.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

