","The correct answer is $\boxed{(c) \text{ZnO}}$.  

**Explanation:**  
- The reduction of metal oxides by carbon is determined by the relative reduction potentials of the metal and carbon. Carbon can reduce oxides of metals with a higher reduction potential than it has.  
- The reduction potential of carbon is $\boxed{-0.74 \text{ V}}$.  
- The reduction potentials of the given metal oxides are:  
  - $\text{Al}_2\text{O}_3$: $\text{Al}^{3+} + 3e^- \rightarrow \text{Al}$ (Reduction potential is $\boxed{-0.44 \text{ V}}$).  
  - $\text{PbO}$: $\text{Pb}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Pb}$ (Reduction potential is $\boxed{-0.25 \text{ V}}$).  
  - $\text{ZnO}$: $\text{Zn}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Zn}$ (Reduction potential is $\boxed{-0.76 \text{ V}}$).  
  - $\text{Fe}_2\text{O}_3$: $\text{Fe}^{3+} + 3e^- \rightarrow \text{Fe}$ (Reduction potential is $\boxed{-0.77 \text{ V}}$).  
- Since the reduction potential of $\text{ZnO}$ and $\text{Fe}_2\text{O}_3$ are more negative than that of carbon, carbon cannot reduce them. However, $\text{ZnO}$ is more difficult to reduce than $\text{Fe}_2\text{O}_3}$, so it is the correct choice.  
- Thus, $\text{ZnO}$ cannot be reduced by carbon.  

