","a) **Series System:**
   - A series system is one where the system fails if one of its components fails.  
   - Let \( R_i(t) \) be the reliability of the \( i \)-th component, where \( i = 1, 2, \ldots, n \).  
   - The system reliability \( R_S(t) \) is the product of the individual component reliabilities:
     \[
     R_S(t) = R_1(t) \times R_2(t) \times R_3(t) \times \ldots \times R_n(t) = \prod_{i=1}^{n} R_i(t)
     \]

b) **Parallel System:**
   - A parallel system is one where the system operates if at least one component is operating.  
   - Let \( R_i(t) \) be the reliability of the \( i \)-th component, where \( i = 1, 2, \ldots, n \).  
   - The system reliability \( R_S(t) \) is the complement of the probability that all components fail:
     \[
     R_S(t) = 1 - \prod_{i=1}^{n} (1 - R_i(t))
     \]
     This is because the system works if at least one component works, and the probability of at least one component working is \( 1 - \text{probability of all components failing} \).

c) **Combination of Serial and Parallel Structures:**
   - For a system with mixed serial and parallel components, the reliability is derived by breaking the system into smaller components (series and parallel) and combining their reliabilities accordingly.  

   - **Example:** Consider a system with two parallel components, each of which is followed by a single component in series.  
     - Let \( R_1(t) \) and \( R_2(t) \) be the reliabilities of the parallel components.  
