At time \( t \), \( a \) travels \( 10t \) km.  

Since \( b \) starts 3 hours after \( a \), \( b \) starts at \( t = 3 \).  
Thus, \( b \) cycles for \( t - 3 \) hours.  
At time \( t \), \( b \) travels \( 20(t - 3) \) km.  

When \( b \) catches up with \( a \), they are at the same distance from the starting point.  
Thus, \( 10t = 20(t - 3) \).  

Solving for \( t \):  
\[ 10t = 20t - 60 \]  
\[ 10t = 60 \]  
\[ t = 6 \text{ hours} \]  

Since \( t = 6 \), \( a \) is at a distance of \( 10 \times 6 = 60 \) km from the starting point.  

