Given $f(x-1) > f(3-2x)$, since $f(x)$ is increasing on $[0,\infty)$, we must have $x-1 > 3-2x$.  

Solving for $x$:  
\[
x - 1 > 3 - 2x \implies x + 2x > 3 + 1 \implies 3x > 4 \implies x > \frac{4}{3}.
\]  

However, we must ensure that $x-1 \geq 0$ and $3-2x \geq 0$.  

\[
x \geq 1 \quad \text{and} \quad 3 \geq 2x \implies x \leq \frac{3}{2}.
\]  

Thus, $1 \leq x \leq \frac{3}{2}$.  

Combining $x > \frac{4}{3}$ and $1 \leq x \leq \frac{3}{2}$, we get $\frac{4}{3} < x \leq \1.5$.  

