Using the identities $\cos (180^\circ + \alpha) = -\cos \alpha$, $\sin (\alpha + 360^\circ) = \sin \alpha$, $\sin (-\alpha - 180^\circ) = \sin (\alpha + 180^\circ) = -\sin \alpha$, and $\cos (-180^\circ - \alpha) = \cos (180^\circ + \alpha) = -\cos \alpha$, the expression simplifies to:
\[
\dfrac{(-\cos \alpha) \cdot (\sin \alpha)}{(-\sin \alpha) \cdot (-\cos \alpha)} = \dfrac{-2 \cos \alpha \sin \alpha}{2 \cos \alpha \sin \alpha} = 1.
\]
