","(a) The acceleration of the proton is given by the force on the proton divided by its mass. The force on the proton is given by the product of the charge and the electric field:  
\[
F = qE = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}) (500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}.
\]  
The acceleration is then:  
\[
a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2.
\]  
Thus, the magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2}\).

(b) The time it takes to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\) is given by the kinematic equation:  
\[
v = v_0 + at,
\]  
where \(v\) is the final velocity, \(v_0\) is the initial velocity, \(a\) is the acceleration, and \(t\) is the time. Since the proton starts from rest, \(v_0 = 0\). Solving for \(t\):  
\[
t = \frac{v - v_0}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s} - 0}{4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2} = 7.2 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{s}.
\]  
Thus, the time it takes to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\) is \(\boxed{7.2 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{s}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
