A spaceship is moving at a speed of 0.6c relative to Earth. Two clocks are on board the ship - one in the cockpit and one in the cargo bay. If the clock in the cockpit shows a time of 3 years when the ship returns to Earth, what will be the time shown on the clock in the cargo bay when it is compared to a clock on Earth? Use the concept of time dilation to calculate the answer.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The time dilation formula is given by:

\[
t = \frac{t_0}{\sqrt{1 - \left(\frac{v^2}{c^2}\right)}}
\]

where:
- \(t\) is the time observed by the Earth observer,
- \(t_0\) is the time observed by the moving object (the clock on the ship),
- \(v\) is the relative velocity between the Earth and the spaceship,
- \(c\) is the speed of light.

Given:
- \(v = 0.6c\)
- \(t_0 = 3 \text{ years}\) (time on the clock in the carload)

First, calculate the time \(t\) observed by Earth for the clock in the carload:

\[
t = \frac{3 \text{ years}}{\sqrt{1 - \left(\frac{(0.6c)^2}{c^2}\right)}} = \frac{3 \text{ years}}{\sqrt{1 - 0.36}} = \frac{3 \text{ years}}{\sqrt{0.64}} = \frac{3 \text{ years}}{0.8} = 3.75 \text{ years}
\]

Thus, the clock in the carload shows 3 years, but the Earth observer observes 3.75 years. 

The time difference between the clock in the carload and the clock in the carload is negligible because the clocks are in the same inertial frame of reference. 

The time on the clock in the carload is \(\boxed{3.75 \text{ years}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

